// TCP for programs (components G01, G03): what the network stack provides
// to everything that talks over the network.

/// TCP connections, listened for and made.
///
/// Provided by the network stack (`netd`). The calls are the control path:
/// listening, accepting, connecting and letting go. Bytes do not travel as
/// calls. A caller lends the stack memory of its own once (`add_memory`),
/// and each connection's two byte streams (`silica_rt::ring`'s
/// `StreamProducer` and `StreamConsumer`; the `silica_net::tcp` crate
/// module wraps all of this) lie in a block of it the caller names when it
/// accepts or connects, so that a connection costs the stack no mapping
/// and a server's millionth connection is as cheap as its first:
///
/// - a block is 2^k bytes, k from 12 to 24 (4 KiB to 16 MiB), at an offset
///   in the memory that is a multiple of its size, its two headers (bytes
///   0 to 127) zeroed by the caller before it names it; what the data
///   areas hold does not matter, since each side reads only bytes the
///   other published by its position;
/// - bytes 0 to 63 of a block are the receive stream's header (the stack
///   writes, the caller reads), bytes 64 to 127 the transmit stream's (the
///   caller writes, the stack reads);
/// - the rest is split in two equal data areas of C = (2^k - 128) / 2
///   bytes: the receive stream's from byte 128, the transmit stream's
///   from byte 128 + C.
///
/// Listeners and connections are their caller's: another program that
/// names one gets `no_such`.
///
/// The stack signals the caller's notification (`wake`) when it put bytes
/// in the receive stream, made room in the transmit stream, or ended the
/// receive stream, each only when the caller said in the stream's header
/// that it sleeps (the streams' own protocol). The caller signals the
/// notification it got back (`requests`) when it wrote to the transmit
/// stream or closed it, likewise only when the stack said it sleeps.
///
/// Ending: the caller closes its transmit stream (reason 0) when it has
/// nothing more to send, and the stack sends what is left, then a FIN. The
/// stack closes the receive stream when the peer has nothing more to send
/// (reason 0, after the last byte), or when the connection failed, with a
/// `Reason`. The caller then lets go of the connection (`close`), which
/// resets it if it had not ended in order. Nothing a caller does with its
/// streams can reach another connection or the stack's state: the stack
/// copies bytes out of and into them and checks every position it reads.
///
/// What a caller may do is in its capability, given by the service manager
/// from the service's declaration (`silica_protocols::scope`): the one port
/// it may listen on (`listen=<port>`) and whether it may connect out
/// (`connect`). A port is the owner's to give, never the first claimant's.
protocol silica.net.Tcp version 2 {
    /// Why a call was refused.
    enum Error : u32 {
        /// The memory is not a usable frame, or is already added
        /// (`add_memory`); or the block named lies outside it, is
        /// misaligned or of a size not allowed, or overlaps another
        /// connection's (`accept`, `connect`).
        bad_memory = 1,
        /// Something already listens on that port.
        in_use = 2,
        /// No connection is waiting to be accepted: wait for the
        /// listener's notification.
        would_block = 3,
        /// No listener or connection has that number.
        no_such = 4,
        /// The stack has no room for another listener or connection.
        no_room = 5,
        /// The stack has no address yet (no interface, or DHCP has not
        /// given one).
        no_address = 6,
        /// A port or address that cannot be used (port 0, address 0).
        bad_argument = 7,
        /// The caller's capability does not carry that port (`listen`) or
        /// connecting out (`connect`).
        not_granted = 8,
    }

    /// Why the stack ended a receive stream other than in order: the
    /// stream's close reason.
    enum Reason : u32 {
        /// The peer sent its FIN: everything it sent has arrived.
        finished = 0,
        /// The peer reset the connection.
        reset = 1,
        /// Nobody listens at the address and port connected to.
        refused = 2,
        /// The peer stopped answering.
        timed_out = 3,
        /// The caller broke a stream's header (a position that makes no
        /// sense): the connection was reset.
        broken = 4,
    }

    /// An IPv4 address and a port.
    struct Endpoint {
        /// The address, first byte highest (10.0.2.2 is 0x0A000202).
        address: u32,
        port: u16,
    }

    /// Lend the stack `memory` (a frame of 64 KiB to 256 MiB, read and
    /// written) for the caller's connections. A caller adds memory once.
    /// When it takes the memory back (revoking it, or ending), the stack
    /// lets go of everything the caller had: its listeners, their ports
    /// free again, and its connections, reset. A second `add_memory` from
    /// the same caller is taken as a new instance of it (a service started
    /// again), and everything the last one had is let go of first.
    add_memory @6 (memory: cap<Frame>) -> () error Error;

    /// Listen for connections to `port` on every address of the machine,
    /// the port the caller's capability carries (`not_granted` otherwise).
    /// The stack completes the handshakes itself and keeps up to `backlog`
    /// (at least 1) connections waiting, signalling `ready` when one
    /// arrives; more arrive refused. Returns the listener's number.
    listen @1 (port: u16, backlog: u32, ready: cap<Notification>) -> (listener: u32) error Error;

    /// Take the oldest connection waiting on `listener`, its streams in
    /// the block of 2^`bits` bytes at `offset` in the caller's memory, the
    /// notification `wake` signalled for it (see above). `would_block`
    /// when none is waiting.
    accept @2 (listener: u32, offset: u64, bits: u8, wake: cap<Notification>) -> (connection: u32, peer: Endpoint, requests: cap<Notification>) error Error;

    /// Connect to `peer` (a capability that carries `connect`), the streams
    /// in the block of 2^`bits` bytes at `offset`, `wake` signalled.
    /// Returns at once: the transmit stream
    /// may be written now and its bytes go once the connection is made; a
    /// connection that cannot be made ends the receive stream with its
    /// `Reason`.
    connect @3 (peer: Endpoint, offset: u64, bits: u8, wake: cap<Notification>) -> (connection: u32, requests: cap<Notification>) error Error;

    /// Let go of `connection`. If the caller ended its transmit stream and
    /// left nothing received unread, the connection ends in order: what it
    /// wrote is sent, then the FIN, as a socket closed on Linux does;
    /// otherwise it is reset. Answered `would_block` while bytes the caller
    /// wrote before ending its stream are not all taken yet (the peer's
    /// window is shut): the caller keeps the block and asks again after the
    /// stack signals the connection's `wake`, which it does when it has
    /// taken the last of them (`silica_net::tcp` does both). Once answered,
    /// the block and the notification are no longer used: the caller may
    /// use the block for another connection.
    close @4 (connection: u32) -> () error Error;

    /// Stop listening; connections still waiting are reset.
    unlisten @5 (listener: u32) -> () error Error;
}
